#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e77e3fa-7a98-5a4a-9335-fb5a9f98bf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272515} {:answer/id #uuid "906a19fb-54f7-5ead-a262-6733d9f69200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273642} {:answer/id #uuid "9846b861-4f4c-519d-bc42-8a2277d230d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273708} {:answer/id #uuid "afdd8e19-abad-54b2-bfa7-482a77bcdc82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273909} {:answer/id #uuid "df16c227-374f-5cca-a61d-8e29808d4fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274355}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273909} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9e1947f-3803-54c7-8228-b7ab54db07e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0ed05f1b-222f-5d8f-b307-8248e81b3d7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-157692" :question/problem "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x+y}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{y}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x},\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n{e}^{-y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121b603b-942f-556e-b445-2d681ee1ce18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+C}{2}\\right)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348602} {:answer/id #uuid "500546dd-32e0-5461-ae4c-5e5a7455b919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349173} {:answer/id #uuid "6e22fd1e-737c-5a35-9366-28fb7f8a36bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=({x}^{2}+x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349458} {:answer/id #uuid "da4666f1-e8ca-5840-a41a-ead889cfbef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=({x}^{2}+x+C)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350361} {:answer/id #uuid "f06bf25f-3d03-5f76-a7ea-8ddc6224884a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x}{2}\\right)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350551}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348602} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e1d264b-d2af-5bb5-bbc0-5706b21c43ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "679701a5-b0c3-5865-adfd-e49e046da668" :question/math-academy-id "q-8303" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}-\\sqrt{y}=2x\\sqrt{y}$ for $y\\ne 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{y} & ={x}^{2}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{y}={x}^{2}+x+C\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+C}{2}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069533 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "610604c1-be04-5ae1-8759-b50654e72419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=K{e}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349333} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4f4cea-17dd-5832-b0a8-70627738c306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349434} {:answer/id #uuid "6c66c47e-a365-5d7e-8cc6-1deb04431c20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349447} {:answer/id #uuid "759561ba-3f4a-5157-8283-98918b52bdbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=K{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349529} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2e52a7-5613-5624-ab39-0ecff616a3c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=K{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350372}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350372} :answer-field/id #uuid "26a2711c-7312-535e-b8ec-1d2da18482c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b946e806-cd3a-503b-8b7c-fe17b0906080" :question/math-academy-id "q-8305" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y=0$ for $y\\ne 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ and $K>0.$\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny=K{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078337 :question/id #uuid "c47d5c55-80f0-5093-810b-bf9c718c5bb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6587" :question/problem "Consider the equation $y''-3y'-4y = 0$ where $y=y(t).$ If $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then what is $\\lambda?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda t},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}-4{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 -  3\\lambda - 4 = 0\n$$\n\nFactoring, we get\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4) (\\lambda +1) = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = 4$ and $\\lambda =  -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078338 :question/id #uuid "2065790f-428d-5477-ac77-0c5d07853143" :question/math-academy-id "e-6588" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y =0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This is a second-order homogeneous ODE. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=e^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = 1$ and $\\lambda = -2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078339 :question/id #uuid "1c7b0878-a386-5449-9ff4-c37c8cda1b92" :question/math-academy-id "e-6589" :question/problem "Given that $y = y(x)$ satisfies the equation $y''+7y'+6y = 0,$ which of the following could **not** be $y(x)?$\n\nI. $-3e^{-6x}$\n\nII. $2e^{-6x}-4e^{-x}$\n\nIII. $e^{-6x}+2e^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "This is a second-order homogeneous ODE. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=e^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+7\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)(\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = -6$ and $\\lambda = - 1.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-6x} + Be^{- x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $A=-3$ and $B=0.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II takes the above form with $A=2$ and $B=-1.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III does *not* take the above form. It contains $e^x,$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078340 :question/id #uuid "8012ecda-d2f4-52de-99de-7b2ebd635661" :question/math-academy-id "e-6676" :question/problem "A loaf of bread just taken out of the oven has a temperature of $180^{\\circ}\\textrm{C}.$ After placing it on the table, the temperature $T(t)$ of the bread decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is $25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the bread's temperature? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $T_S = 25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - 25).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078341 :question/id #uuid "e3e001a8-9294-59a4-82e6-b1122afdc72f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6677" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a fluorescent light bulb after turning off can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23)\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the bulb is $80^\\circ \\rm C,$ calculate the temperature of the bulb after $4$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the fluorescent light bulb is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-0.25\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-\\int 0.25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-23| & =-0.25t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T - 23 > 0$ for all $t > 0.$ \nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-23 & ={e}^{-0.25t+C} \\\\ T-23 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T-23 & =K{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T & =23+K{e}^{-0.25t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the light bulb was $80^\\circ \\rm C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=80,$ and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}80 & =23+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 80 & =23+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t) = 23+57e^{-0.25t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the bulb after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4) = 23+57e^{-0.25\\cdot 4}\\approx 44^\\circ\\textrm{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078374 :question/id #uuid "9d7fbff5-7db5-53e4-a17e-3fb7ac7a0069" :question/math-academy-id "e-8957" :question/problem "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\nI. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y $\n\nII. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y $\n\nIII. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -k(y - a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y > a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, II is the only inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -12(y-50).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=12,$ and $a=50.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y > 50$ for all $t > 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d0731f6-6530-57d1-8101-d583dce27200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238171} {:answer/id #uuid "30cfb7a5-89a3-5976-92cb-41428186daaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238479} {:answer/id #uuid "afd882b4-452c-52f4-9d6a-2ad9a9e823b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2y(y-500),\\,y(0)=400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239582} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0874aa-3afc-56be-b514-dbaa87daff29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y(y-500),\\,y(0)=600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239832} {:answer/id #uuid "d2980861-6b7b-5001-a205-b1b4d310530b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239903}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238479} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2b7af48-1a47-5f7e-aa94-0ab3a1a923bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7fb765b0-7266-558a-8445-d5e7e8dcfb07" :question/math-academy-id "q-105890" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=2,a=500,$ and ${y}_{0}=600.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=400,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=400<a=500.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e3800c8-cb88-5193-ac78-ecad629bac8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 800y - 20y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075107} {:answer/id #uuid "cc86f27c-9102-5471-8151-edb2db7a3851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 20y^{2} - 800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075108} {:answer/id #uuid "de0a15f9-0ee2-5d22-9af1-22fcb5891d6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 800 - 20y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075109} {:answer/id #uuid "34c6d6ee-08e6-51ad-b478-11481d79e6d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 800 + 20y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075110} {:answer/id #uuid "6537327b-2aa9-5b4f-8d32-77ba1961661c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 800 - 20y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075111}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c6cb1533-9217-5c12-ab91-a8596f52e7b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075106}] :question/id #uuid "efe4332d-8d00-5d94-9cfa-87582b5e92ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-105893" :question/problem "Given that $y > 40$ and $t > 0$, which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc34311-544b-58ac-b0d4-5ffec4e304fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=80(y+20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238574} {:answer/id #uuid "4e20476d-f9bb-5130-8f9e-628e39957f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80y(20-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238728} {:answer/id #uuid "565d5a3d-b07c-5417-aca5-0f14a0b69ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(20-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238800} {:answer/id #uuid "90d5ff36-f97d-5e89-b7ec-2b83f4fa6dfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=80y(y+20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239310} {:answer/id #uuid "e37d7ad7-c270-5174-be15-143df61c1f64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(y-20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240055}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240055} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2372051-f6b8-5f84-9685-5fee700182de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f0fe545c-1008-5ec6-a573-a706bc6b026b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105898" :question/problem "Given that $y>20$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(y-20).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=80,$ and $a=20.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>20$ for all $t>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72fd79b0-8552-59fe-b722-7e484092a69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445938} {:answer/id #uuid "a014bd72-72d0-51e6-aca9-a65a27712eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-kT(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445940} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9d954e-d7a7-5d73-9350-3b59b8993edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=k(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445941} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c15d21-0615-53b3-b41f-f6213357630d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T+25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445946} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ceeca0-5ae8-54f6-8a11-ae6c66b94346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445947}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445938} :answer-field/id #uuid "385b574a-86cb-5cbc-a02b-25339e6092c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "246dbe47-9137-5b96-af87-04ddded3bed6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105917" :question/problem "The rate at which a liquid cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the liquid in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-25).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1deaf31a-b94f-5dbd-92e7-025ade20cd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272654} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7840ac-4dc2-5743-a317-909d0854aa2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273200} {:answer/id #uuid "80a9183c-594a-50ff-89ee-e5298cd3be92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273490} {:answer/id #uuid "87fc2c9b-f809-527a-9228-06e37aeb1131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273553} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fc440f-ad27-570f-b514-432bec694cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274578}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272654} :answer-field/id #uuid "d15396bb-b0f6-5ba4-9f17-f68ca77b61da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8b0ed3c5-b3aa-5668-ae2c-48b159839332" :question/math-academy-id "q-15384" :question/problem "A thermometer is placed into a water bath and displays a reading of ${50}^{∘}C.$ The thermometer is then transferred to a ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath, and its reading begins to decrease. After $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${30}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred into the ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={10}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-10).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-10| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-10>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-10 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-10 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-10 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =10+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${50}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=50,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & =10+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 50 & =10+K \\\\ K & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=10+40{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=40,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =10+40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 30 & =40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 10+40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =20 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}≈12\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1495237b-afbf-5205-a022-9f6acc5cbda9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{83}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272570} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8e38af-c5d0-507f-a1bd-aae08b27500d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{79}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273439} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f377d8-6bb6-5509-84d5-efce6120f034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{85}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273947} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd7cc63-77c2-5834-8ea5-22b666b6543c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274316} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb4ed67-f97a-54dc-81d8-37f939ad8898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{77}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274619}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274316} :answer-field/id #uuid "217e660e-5140-5d1b-956d-f278238bb223" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "daa15db5-c1a1-535c-9031-79b2bb1e03e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-15595" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cup of coffee can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the coffee is ${100}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the coffee after $6$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the cup of coffee is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-0.05\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-\\int 0.05\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-0.05t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-25>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-25 & ={e}^{-0.05t+C} \\\\ T-25 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T-25 & =K{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T & =25+K{e}^{-0.05t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the coffee was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =25+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =25+K \\\\ K & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=25+75{e}^{-0.05t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the coffee after $t=6$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(6)=25+75{e}^{-0.05\\cdot 6}≈{81}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "342581f4-cabc-5059-a251-2692ca6f27ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-64(T-100)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321542} {:answer/id #uuid "71be98e8-1e44-50bb-9bb9-b32dd2edc859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-100(T-64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322040} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e6a8e3-742a-59bc-850f-65fe5a3e9e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-100)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322796} {:answer/id #uuid "e62e99ed-fb8f-592b-af00-7b9afa2790e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-164)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322949} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9ef3f2-b765-5498-b81e-dd99e9dc25d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323010}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323010} :answer-field/id #uuid "53b01d91-18e1-599b-8abf-64d7291ac4f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "58a15628-6a07-582f-a2d4-e1c7a7c6e77c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37365" :question/problem "A medical doctor records the temperature of ${100}^{∘}\\text{F}$ using a thermometer. After the measurement is taken, the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the thermometer reading? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079159 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d353bf23-fca7-52bf-a7f3-afd4ed20c947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-3$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077733} {:answer/id #uuid "d487b092-ee9a-57c6-b9b0-84830dba4996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-5$ or $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077734} {:answer/id #uuid "2c18d718-4433-5823-a1ec-47e3df9987d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ or $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077735} {:answer/id #uuid "383dd0ef-57a7-596a-b378-20cd37ec74ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-2$ or $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077736} {:answer/id #uuid "397c3e01-02ba-5858-89ff-549b7f481fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ or $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077737}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ff85455f-e71e-5059-ad3d-03d365403032" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077732}] :question/id #uuid "e4c1d720-dc57-57ee-9e14-9f4a87e7e17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-64658" :question/problem "Consider the equation $y^{″} - 5y^{′} + 6y = 0$ where $y = y(x)$. If $y = e^{λx}$ is a solution, then $λ =$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079160 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a0b1d01-fdb0-586f-851e-92f612fa8195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077739} {:answer/id #uuid "99c73e0d-2724-51f3-bd81-32248af71bd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5e^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077740} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0d3275-316d-5442-80dd-540cedb8a306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077741} {:answer/id #uuid "b5df64b7-477d-5af9-8cf8-067d22153384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{6x} - 2e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077742} {:answer/id #uuid "cc314592-0237-5d57-837e-d318c8f94321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e^{6x} + e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077743}] :answer-field/id #uuid "44ad0bcb-b98c-5662-9e8b-8b209506e628" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077738}] :question/id #uuid "93a3e693-dee3-519b-b3b7-c74034cf9ec3" :question/math-academy-id "q-64659" :question/problem "Given that $y = y(x)$ satisfies the equation $y^{″} - 5y^{′} - 6y = 0$, which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4bfcad9-bbdb-5f36-ab0d-96bb7cc51915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ or $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077745} {:answer/id #uuid "18a0b43d-6412-51ab-9756-595dc45edd87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ or $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077746} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb462ff-e780-5406-bb45-3055760adea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077747} {:answer/id #uuid "538b52db-3f93-564d-8a95-13c3ebff6bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-6$ or $-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077748} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad1ea81-2863-5a5e-bdce-939e8d691ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-1$ or $-9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077749}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ba63ca86-06f5-5c2f-b048-c19ca85d09f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077744}] :question/id #uuid "c82f5e4a-6b3d-5d26-aaf5-03917457f19e" :question/math-academy-id "q-64704" :question/problem "Consider the equation $y^{″} + 9y^{′} + 18y = 0$ where $y = y(x)$. If $y = e^{λx}$ is a solution, then $λ =$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079162 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "221e1e12-fd82-5746-bf0f-8371e73f431f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-4t}+{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333295} {:answer/id #uuid "468f21aa-b994-5cfb-9b54-4f931e72bcf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-4t}-{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333642} {:answer/id #uuid "702263ab-234b-55d2-9849-84e6a43e97f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334011} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b50263-23ea-593c-b199-69d4a7a58e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{-4t}+{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334540} {:answer/id #uuid "ef772abb-ba2a-5c3e-937a-89bc6a8a3afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335087}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333295} :answer-field/id #uuid "489185f8-eaf8-58ca-a92d-5b178a2e959a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9f5e7eb7-af07-5752-88d0-e6c5860716ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-64707" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}-4{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =1.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-4t}$ and ${e}^{t}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $-{e}^{-4t}+{e}^{-t}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bec9d70-f8de-51cf-9e64-a849b809ce13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342607} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0ed665-7b86-5adb-85fc-2c91f4201111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343490} {:answer/id #uuid "c479827f-1f07-57f4-80cf-2fa9049345a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{6x}+B{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343966} {:answer/id #uuid "c65f01c2-eff5-56e3-8d94-e7a8e6fe2b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343978} {:answer/id #uuid "f51f0ae1-02ed-5b8f-a1ff-80e0e5ce8f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344396}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342607} :answer-field/id #uuid "c56404db-2432-58f9-a3d9-cc82f8d56420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b4af7b02-aa3b-5c89-83b9-f0034ff7803a" :question/math-academy-id "q-7781" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+8{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +4)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =-4.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a0f9485-72e1-575a-8d39-c36d01a1f161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-x} + Be^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078021} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ca355c-f3ca-5bf5-88e1-18687f27cdef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-2x} + Be^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078022} {:answer/id #uuid "b3692ed3-4e64-5eae-a73a-687b43469fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-2x} + Be^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078023} {:answer/id #uuid "0650080f-1d71-54af-ab95-4f8958346796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-x} + Be^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078024} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccb8a32-49dd-5721-bfb7-5cbc467168e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y = Ae^{2x} + Be^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078025}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5f233c7e-f0db-504e-95a6-8675a27393c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078020}] :question/id #uuid "b5dcdff1-57ea-5744-bf62-658396778f6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-7784" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 3\\frac{dy}{dx} - 10y = 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087456 :question/id #uuid "34c357f1-505b-530f-8bb4-376eada248a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2301" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y = vx$  (where $v=v(x)$) to rewrite the given first-order homogeneous ODE \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}    =   \\cos \\left( \\frac {y} {x} \\right)\n$$\n\nas a separable ODE in $v$ and $x.$" :question/worked-solution "With $y = vx$ (where $v = v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging that into the differential equation and separating variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087457 :question/id #uuid "89b7276c-ef41-5123-ad31-b54753656111" :question/math-academy-id "e-2302" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\frac{x^2+y^2}{2xy}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We note that this equation is a homogeneous first-order differential equation because\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}f ( t x , t y ) &  = \\frac{( t x {)}^{2} + ( t y {)}^{2}}{2 ( t x ) ( t y )} \\\\  &  = \\frac{{t}^{2} {x}^{2} + {t}^{2} {y}^{2}}{2 {t}^{2} x y} \\\\  &  = \\frac{{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}{2 x y} \\\\  &  = f ( x , y ) .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe given equation is not separable, but we can transform it into a separable equation by carrying out the substitution\n$$\ny=vx, \\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = v + x \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} \\, .\n$$\n\nCarrying out the substitution and separating variables, we have\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} & \\displaystyle  = \\frac{ {x}^{2} + {y}^{2}}{2 x y} \\\\ \\displaystyle v + x {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} v}{\\text{d} x}} & \\displaystyle  = \\frac{ {x}^{2} + ( v x {)}^{2}}{2 x ( v x )} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\frac{ {x}^{2} + {v}^{2} {x}^{2}}{2 {x}^{2} v} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\frac{ {x}^{2} ( 1 + {v}^{2} )}{2 {x}^{2} v} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\frac{1 + {v}^{2}}{2 v} \\\\ \\displaystyle x {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} v}{\\text{d} x}} & \\displaystyle  = \\frac{1 + {v}^{2}}{2 v} - v \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\frac{1 + {v}^{2}}{2 v} - \\frac{2 {v}^{2}}{2 v} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\frac{1 - {v}^{2}}{2 v} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\frac{2 v}{1 - {v}^{2}} \\frac{\\text{d} v}{\\text{d} x} & \\displaystyle  = \\frac{1}{x} \\hspace{0.167em} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides, we get\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\int  \\frac{2 v}{1 - {v}^{2}} \\frac{\\text{d} v}{\\text{d} x} \\text{d} x &  = \\int  \\frac{1}{x} \\text{d} x \\\\ \\int  \\frac{2 v}{1 - {v}^{2}} \\text{d} v &  = \\ln   | x | + {C}_{1} \\\\ - \\ln   | 1 - {v}^{2} | &  = \\ln   | {C}_{2} x | \\\\ \\ln   ∣ \\frac{1}{1 - {v}^{2}} ∣ &  = \\ln   | {C}_{2} x | \\\\ ∣ \\frac{1}{1 - {v}^{2}} ∣ &  = | {C}_{2} x | \\\\ \\frac{1}{1 - {v}^{2}} &  = \\pm  {C}_{2} x \\\\ 1 - {v}^{2} &  = \\frac{\\pm  1}{{C}_{2} x} \\\\ 1 - {v}^{2} &  = \\frac{{C}_{3}}{x}\\end{array}\n$$\nwhere $C_1=\\ln{C_2}$ (with ${C}_{2} > 0$) and ${C}_{3} = \\pm  \\frac{1}{{C}_{2}} .$ \n\nFinally, substituting $v=\\dfrac{y}{x}$ and simplifying, we get\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 1 - {v}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = \\frac{ {C}_{3}}{x} \\\\ \\displaystyle 1 - {( \\frac{y}{x} )}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = \\frac{ {C}_{3}}{x} \\\\ \\displaystyle 1 - \\frac{ {y}^{2}}{ {x}^{2}} & \\displaystyle  = \\frac{ {C}_{3}}{x} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\frac{ {y}^{2}}{ {x}^{2}} & \\displaystyle  = 1 - \\frac{ {C}_{3}}{x} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\frac{ {y}^{2}}{ {x}^{2}} & \\displaystyle  = 1 + \\frac{ {C}_{4}}{x} \\\\ \\displaystyle {y}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = {x}^{2} ( 1 + \\frac{ {C}_{4}}{x} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle {y}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = {x}^{2} + {C}_{4} x\\end{array}\n$$\nwhere $C_4 = -C_3.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {y}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = {x}^{2} + {C}_{4} x .\\end{array}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087458 :question/id #uuid "f34de8cd-b650-5926-9f06-8127d4fb3a55" :question/math-academy-id "e-2393" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0.$ Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1\\mathbin{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{{t}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}\n$$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{2(tx)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot 2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087515 :question/id #uuid "446ea007-1ff4-5023-8e3f-7f00eb0fb2d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5071" :question/problem "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](images/6d/6d2ba3fba1a05a841a486553ea3a52d6f3797974630420ad79ae50f4c6f5bc15.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087516 :question/id #uuid "af56117c-65e0-5f12-9780-7a95e6be829e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5072" :question/problem "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](images/3e/3e8d5ed4bf055960b50e046c0688a3c4cc69907ebb1209bb9658ac4bcba48ffe.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087517 :question/id #uuid "b7c94673-f847-5977-9b9f-8d235ea42eb2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5073" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation $\\dfrac{x^2}{36} + \\dfrac{y^2}{25} = 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $a^2=36$ and $b^2=25.$\n\n- Since $a^2=36,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{36} = 6.$\n\n- Since $b^2=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25} = 5.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=5.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a = 2 \\cdot 6 = 12,$ and the minor axis has length $2b = 2 \\cdot 5 =10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087561 :question/id #uuid "7ec4eaee-0872-55a3-abef-1b66f9d43969" :question/math-academy-id "e-542" :question/problem "Suppose that the population $P(t)$ of a town after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}P} {\\textrm{d}t} = 0.02P.\n$$\n\nIf there are currently $500$ people in the town, what will be the town's population in $3$ years?" :question/worked-solution "We note that this is an exponential growth differential equation with $r = 0.02$. Therefore, the solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = P_0\\,e^{0.02t},\n$$\n\nwhere $P_0$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the population is currently $500,$ so we have $P_0 = 500.$ \n\nTherefore, the population of the town at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nP(t) = 500\\,e^{0.02t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the population after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =500\\,{e}^{0.02\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =500\\,{e}^{0.06} \\\\  & ≈531\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the population will be approximately $531$ after $3$ years."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087622 :question/id #uuid "f9e8b643-2650-5481-9210-46e796417449" :question/math-academy-id "e-7273" :question/problem "The number of followers of a celebrity's social media account increases at a rate that is directly proportional to the number of followers at that time. Given that there are currently $600\\,000$ followers and in $3$ months there will be $780\\,000$ followers, what is the growth rate? Round your answer to the nearest percent." :question/worked-solution "The rate $N'(t)$ at which the number of followers increases is directly proportional to the number of followers $N.$ Therefore, we can model the number of followers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} N}{\\mathrm{d} t} = rN,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is measured in months, and $r > 0$ is the growth rate.\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t) = N_0\\,e^{rt},\n$$\n\nwhere $N_0$ is the initial number of followers.\n\nWe're told that there are currently $600\\,000$ followers, so we have $N_0 = 600\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of followers $N$ after $t$ months is\n\n$$\nN(t) = 600\\,000 \\, e^{rt} .\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the growth rate, we substitute $N(3)=780\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =600\\,000\\,{e}^{r\\cdot 3} \\\\ 780\\,000 & =600\\,000\\,{e}^{3r} \\\\ {e}^{3r} & =\\frac{780\\,000}{600\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{3r} & =1.3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.3) \\\\ 3r & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.3) \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.3) \\\\ r & ≈0.087\\,54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r\\approx 9\\%,$ rounded to the nearest percent."}]}
